<!--
  a link that leaves the app, set at the end of a link in a sub-line.

  **the smaller of two sizes of one mark, and it is two files because it has to be.** a compound
  drawable is drawn at its own intrinsic size and nothing at the point of use can scale it, so a mark
  that has to sit with two text sizes is two drawables. this one follows the licence line, whose text
  is about a third smaller than the value lines above it; `ic_open_link.xml` is the same shape at the
  size those take.

  **the size is chosen against the cap height it sits beside rather than picked.** one size for both
  lines leaves the mark taller than the capital letters on the smaller of them, which reads as an icon
  dropped into a sentence rather than as punctuation ending it. what the two sizes hold constant is the
  ratio, not the dp.

  Material's `open_in_new`, at Material Symbols' 960 viewport. it carries no colour: the style that
  places it tints it with the same attribute the text takes, so the two cannot drift.

  **the box carries empty space above the glyph, and that is how it is aligned.** a compound drawable
  is centred on the text view's line box, whose centre sits above the text's own optical centre, so
  left alone the mark rides high. nothing at the point of use can move it, but a taller box does:
  adding E of space above the glyph keeps the box centred and therefore moves the glyph down by E/2.
  so the space above is twice the shift wanted, 2.6dp here, measured against the line this mark
  follows rather than guessed.

  **it scales with the text and is not a constant.** the shift comes out of the font's metrics, so a
  different text size wants it measured again rather than copied. `ratio.py` and `align.py` beside this
  project's notes are what measure it.
-->
